  <front>
    <title>Exposition Style</title>
    <seriesInfo name="RFC" value="154" stream="IETF"/>
    <author fullname="S. Crocker" initials="S." surname="Crocker">
      <organization showOnFrontPage="true">UCLA</organization>
      <address/>
    </author>
    <date year="1971" month="May"/>
    <abstract pn="section-abstract">
      <t indent="0" pn="section-abstract-1">As a pedagogical device for describing functions such as the one
below</t>
      <artwork name="" type="" align="left" alt="" pn="section-abstract-2"><![CDATA[
           |
           |                  +-----------------
           |                 /:
           |                / :
           |               /  :
           |              /   :
           |             /    :
           |            /     :
      -----+------------------------------------
           |                  a
           |
]]></artwork>
      <t indent="0" pn="section-abstract-3">where two formulae, f1 and f2, are necessary for adjoining domains but
the function is continous at the boundary point, I usually write the
description in the form</t>
      <artwork name="" type="" align="left" alt="" pn="section-abstract-4"><![CDATA[
        f(x) = f1(x) for x =< a
        f(x) = f2(x) for x >= a
]]></artwork>
      <t indent="0" pn="section-abstract-5">The astute reader will note that the domains overlap, but that
f1(a) = f2(a), so no semantic ambiguity obtains.</t>
      <t indent="0" pn="section-abstract-6">[ This RFC was put into machine readable form for entry ]
[ into the online RFC archives by Naoki Matsuhira 5/97 ]</t>
    </abstract>
